<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Godin Tepe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Godin Tepe is an archaeological site in the Kangavar valley of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Excavations found a walled enclave with Uruk-style pottery and tablets of the late fourth millennium BCE, early chemical evidence of wine and beer, and a fortified manor of the Median period of the eighth to sixth centuries BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Although Deioces' historicity is debated, Assyrian records and excavated sites such as <b>Godin Tepe</b> and Nush-i Jan attest to growing Median power in this period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The city depended on materials it lacked, such as timber, stone and metals, and in the so-called Uruk expansion settlers founded outposts and colonies far up the Euphrates in Syria and south-eastern Anatolia, such as Habuba Kabira and Jebel Aruda, and in western Iran at <b>Godin Tepe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
